WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.694 --> 00:00:23.524
Hey, Little Fox readers. It's me, Alex, again. I just got back from ice hockey practice; our coach showed us how to use the neutral zone trap.

2
00:00:23.525 --> 00:00:28.935
That's a strategy to keep the other team trapped in the middle of the hockey rink.

3
00:00:28.936 --> 00:00:35.607
The trap is supposed to stop opposing players from entering our zone and scoring goals.

4
00:00:35.608 --> 00:00:41.169
But I wanted to tell you about another zone we just learned about from Mr. Fay.

5
00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:48.256
It's the Demilitarized Zone, or DMZ, which separates North and South Korea.

6
00:00:48.257 --> 00:00:58.838
Even though it's referred to as a demilitarized zone, the DMZ is the most heavily fortified border in the world.

7
00:00:58.839 --> 00:01:09.904
You probably already know this, but Korea wasn't always divided into two separate countries. During World War II, Japan controlled Korea.

8
00:01:09.905 --> 00:01:22.546
When Japan lost the war, the Soviet Union and United States temporarily took control of the peninsula, with the Soviet Union in the North and the United Sates in the South.

9
00:01:22.547 --> 00:01:35.385
By 1948 two separate countries had been formed—the Democratic People's Republic of Korea (North Korea) and the Republic of Korea (South Korea).

10
00:01:35.386 --> 00:01:43.631
The two nations were divided along the earth's 38th parallel, or 38 degrees north of the equator.

11
00:01:43.632 --> 00:01:48.701
Each side claimed to be the legitimate government of all of Korea.

12
00:01:48.702 --> 00:01:56.518
Then in 1950 North Korea invaded South Korea, with help from the Soviet Union.

13
00:01:56.519 --> 00:02:03.621
The South fought back, aided by the United States, as well as the United Nations.

14
00:02:03.622 --> 00:02:11.184
Fighting continued for three years, until the two sides finally reached a cease-fire agreement.

15
00:02:11.185 --> 00:02:19.438
The 1953 cease-fire agreement reestablished the border between the two countries near the 38th parallel.

16
00:02:19.439 --> 00:02:26.095
This border became known as the Military Demarcation Line, or MDL.

17
00:02:26.096 --> 00:02:36.504
Both sides pulled back two kilometers from the MDL, leaving a four-kilometer buffer zone—the DMZ—between the two nations.

18
00:02:36.505 --> 00:02:50.407
Today this buffer zone is an extremely dangerous place, with razor wire, watchtowers, heavy artillery, and soldiers lined up along both its northern and southern boundaries.

19
00:02:50.408 --> 00:02:57.726
Soldiers are allowed to patrol inside the DMZ, but they are not allowed to cross the MDL.

20
00:02:57.727 --> 00:03:07.402
The only place in the DMZ where soldiers from the two Koreas face each other is the Joint Security Area (JSA).

21
00:03:07.403 --> 00:03:17.048
Built on the village of Panmunjom, the JSA is bisected by the MDL, which is marked with a low concrete slab.

22
00:03:17.049 --> 00:03:26.221
The JSA contains a small number of buildings, including watchtowers and several low blue buildings owned by the U.N.

23
00:03:26.221 --> 00:03:35.036
Diplomatic talks between North and South Korea are sometimes held in a conference room inside one of these U.N. buildings.

24
00:03:35.037 --> 00:03:48.510
In order to be stationed in the JSA, a South Korean soldier must hold a black belt in either taekwondo or judo and be at least 177 cm tall.

25
00:03:48.511 --> 00:03:55.509
The South Korean soldiers guard the area with fists clenched and stony facial expressions.

26
00:03:55.510 --> 00:04:04.019
They also wear mirrored sunglasses, which prevents them from staring directly into the eyes of North Korean troops.

27
00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:09.001
Mr. Fay said that could lead to a dangerous conflict!

28
00:04:09.002 --> 00:04:14.658
North Korea also puts only highly trained soldiers in the JSA.

29
00:04:14.659 --> 00:04:21.505
Like the South Korean soldiers, the North Korean troops assume a rigid military stance.

30
00:04:21.506 --> 00:04:28.752
Despite the cease-fire agreement, a few violent incidents have occurred in the DMZ over the years.

31
00:04:28.753 --> 00:04:41.240
One especially tragic event happened in 1976, when a small group of South Korean and American soldiers were pruning a tree to improve their view of a checkpoint.

32
00:04:41.241 --> 00:04:49.996
After ordering the troops to stop, North Korean soldiers attacked and murdered two American officers with axes.

33
00:04:49.997 --> 00:04:57.900
To avoid reigniting the war, South Korea and the U.S. decided not to retaliate with military force.

34
00:04:57.901 --> 00:05:06.712
Instead a large group of soldiers returned to the site three days later and cut the tree down completely.

35
00:05:06.713 --> 00:05:12.711
Mr. Fay told us that secret tunnels have also been discovered under the DMZ.

36
00:05:12.712 --> 00:05:24.946
In 1974 a South Korean patrol group discovered the first one, which had electricity, lighting, sleeping areas, and storage areas for weapons.

37
00:05:24.947 --> 00:05:28.863
Since then even more tunnels have been found.

38
00:05:28.864 --> 00:05:37.617
Today the landscape in the DMZ consists of wetlands, forests, mountains, and big open fields.

39
00:05:37.618 --> 00:05:45.356
If it hadn't become the DMZ back in 1953, the land probably would have been developed by now.

40
00:05:45.357 --> 00:05:51.180
But instead the area has become an amazing wildlife sanctuary.

41
00:05:51.181 --> 00:06:01.841
In the DMZ you can see flying squirrels, along with very rare animals like Asiatic black bears and Chinese gorals.

42
00:06:01.842 --> 00:06:11.986
Two of the most endangered bird species in the world—the white-naped crane and the red-crowned crane—have also been spotted here.

43
00:06:11.987 --> 00:06:21.235
By now most of the land in South Korea has been developed, and North Korea's landscape has suffered from severe deforestation.

44
00:06:21.236 --> 00:06:28.974
So environmental groups want to preserve this pristine ecosystem, even if Korea becomes unified again.

45
00:06:28.975 --> 00:06:38.706
The DMZ may be the front line between two countries that are technically still at war, but it has also become a popular tourist destination.

46
00:06:38.707 --> 00:06:50.789
About 100,000 people visit the JSA each year, with tour guides taking many of them into the U.N. conference room and even into the secret tunnels.

47
00:06:50.790 --> 00:06:59.450
If you ever travel to the DMZ, you'll need to remember that soldiers across the MDL are watching you.

48
00:06:59.451 --> 00:07:06.370
You can only look in certain directions, and you need permission before taking pictures.

49
00:07:06.371 --> 00:07:13.037
Under no circumstances can you point or wave at anyone on the other side of the border.

50
00:07:13.038 --> 00:07:21.601
Both sides are ready to fight at a moment's notice, so you have to avoid doing anything that could be misinterpreted!

51
00:07:21.602 --> 00:07:28.275
The DMZ definitely sounds dangerous, but I'd really like to visit it one day.

52
00:07:28.276 --> 00:07:39.667
Mr. Fay described it as one of the most unique and fascinating places in the world—a place where two countries are divided by a line that cannot be crossed.

53
00:07:39.668 --> 00:07:47.334
Well, Little Fox readers, I'd better get ready for bed now. My hockey team has a big game tomorrow.

54
00:07:47.335 --> 00:07:53.555
Hey . . . maybe we'll get a chance to use the neutral zone trap—and win the game!
